<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oba-of-lagos">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oba of Lagos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> is the traditional ruler of Lagos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. The line traces its origin to Ashipa, a war chief recognised by the Oba of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> around the 16th century, and the early Obas of Lagos paid tribute to Benin. They ruled Lagos until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed it in 1861, and the office survives with ceremonial and customary authority, based at the Iga Idunganran palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oba-of-lagos.jpg" alt="Oba of Lagos" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by Journalist 1st Class Kurt Riggs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oba_Lagos060602-N-8637R-006_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
